Q: How would I make a document have two columns in a manner similar to stuff from pathfinder? A: <p>When aiming for a HTML 2 PDF output you need to understand the PDF writers use of units.</p>
<p>Since the majority of web users will be using chromium based browsers, the units are decided by the SKIA save/print as printing driver.</p>
<p>Skia will for a metric page iniate as 803 pixels wide and scale content according to that into 1132 px high which cannot be changed for an A4 page.</p>
<p><code>width=&quot;803&quot; height=&quot;1132&quot;</code></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/KPnTVY8G.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/KPnTVY8G.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Currently the CSS is using 700px for a gap between the columns thus with such a large gap the text can <strong>at most</strong> be 50px wide each side. however due to other setting it will push to the right.
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/r4DG46kZ.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/r4DG46kZ.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>If you want to <strong>enforce</strong> a true &quot;metric&quot; scale page it is wise to use the print media settings in the <code>style</code> as:</p>
<pre><code>@media print { @page { margin: 0; size: 210mm 297mm ; } body { margin: 0; } } 
</code></pre>
<p>However that may not be good for mixed media contents, hence best to work in HTML px</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
@media print {
  @page {
    margin: 0;
    size: 794px 1122px;
  }
  body {
    margin-left: 50px;
    margin-right: 50px;
  }
} 
/* Columns */
div.col-2{
    column-count:2;
    column-gap:25px;
}

/* Font */
......................
</code></pre>
<p>This ensures desired scales and spacing:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/YjR9T67x.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/YjR9T67x.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p><strong>At 25px the gap in the PDF will be about 18.5 points (It should be exactly 18.75 pts, but characters have guarding widths at the side.)</strong></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/kEuyuM6b.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/kEuyuM6b.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>

Q: How to clean up HTML table using CSS solely to make width responsive A: <p>Move all the styling to either:</p>
<ul>
<li>a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/style" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element</a>, or</li>
<li>a separate <code>.css</code> file referenced with a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/link" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>&lt;link&gt;</code> element</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.agent-table {
  width: 100%;
  color: black;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;

  td:first-child {        /* first cell in each row */
    width: 20%;
    font-size: 1.7vw;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  td:last-child {         /* last cell in each row */
    width: 70%; 
    font-size: 1.4vw;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;table class="agent-table"&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Agent 1&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;details of agent 1&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Agent 2&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;details of agent 2&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Agent 3&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;details of agent 3&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Agent 4&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;details of agent 4&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Agent 5&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;details of agent 5&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Agent 6&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;details of agent 6&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Why aren&#39;t justify-items, justify-content, etc working in grid cell alignment? A: <p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
  * {
    list-style-type: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  div,
  li {
    border: 1px solid rgb(142, 28, 47);
  }

  .header {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5fr 1fr 1fr;
  }

  ul {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-top: 10px;
  }
&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;div class="header"&gt;
  &lt;div class="Search"&gt;Search&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="Alert"&gt;Alert&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="User Info"&gt;User Info&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="Intro"&gt;Hi There&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;New&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Upload&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Share&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><code>justify-items</code> controls how items are aligned inside each individual grid cell, not how the items themselves are spaced in the container.
<code>space-between</code> is not a valid value for <code>justify-items</code>.</p>
